# Backend Standards

Priority: Highest

Every prompt in this project must follow this document.

If a prompt conflicts with this document, this document takes precedence unless explicitly stated otherwise.

> This document defines the backend engineering standards for the Sports Companion application.
>
> Every implementation must follow these standards.
> Unless explicitly instructed otherwise, these rules are considered mandatory.

---

# 1. Architecture

The project follows **Clean Architecture**.

```
src
├── API
├── Application
├── Domain
├── Infrastructure
└── Localization
```

Dependencies must always point inward.

```
API
    ↓
Application
    ↓
Domain

Infrastructure
    ↓
Application
    ↓
Domain
```

The Domain layer must never depend on any other layer.

---

# 2. General Principles

- Follow SOLID principles.
- Follow Clean Code principles.
- Follow Domain Driven Design where applicable.
- Keep classes small and focused.
- One class should have one responsibility.
- Avoid premature optimization.
- Prefer composition over inheritance.
- Avoid unnecessary abstractions.
- Never duplicate business logic.

---

# 3. Naming Convention

## Classes

Use PascalCase.

Example

```
User
Event
CreateEventCommand
```

---

## Interfaces

Always start with "I".

```
IUserRepository
IJwtService
```

---

## Methods

Use PascalCase.

Methods should always start with a verb.

```
Create()
Update()
Delete()
JoinEvent()
```

---

## Properties

Use PascalCase.

```
FirstName
CreatedAt
AverageRating
```

---

## Variables

Use camelCase.

```
user
currentUser
eventParticipants
```

---

## Database

Tables use plural `DbSet` names and EF Core conventions.

```
Users
Profiles
Events
Messages
```

Columns

Use entity property names through EF Core conventions.

```
CreatedAt
UpdatedAt
UserId
```

Foreign Keys

```
<entity>_id
```

Example

```
user_id
event_id
profile_id
```

---

# 4. Domain Layer

The Domain layer must not depend on:

- Entity Framework
- ASP.NET Core
- Infrastructure
- External Services

The Domain layer contains only business rules.

---

## Common Structure

```
Domain
└── Common
    ├── Base
    ├── Constants
    ├── Enums
    ├── Exceptions
    └── ValueObjects
```

---

# 5. Base Classes

The project uses the following hierarchy.

```
BaseEntity
      │
      ▼
AuditableEntity
      │
      ▼
AggregateRoot
```

## BaseEntity

Contains only:

- Id

Every entity in the project must inherit from BaseEntity either directly or indirectly.

---

## AuditableEntity

Contains

- CreatedAt
- UpdatedAt
- CreatedByUserId
- UpdatedByUserId

## All entities should inherit from AuditableEntity unless there is a specific reason not to.

## AggregateRoot

Represents aggregate roots in the domain.

Example

```
User
Event
Badge
```

---

# 6. Entity Rules

Every entity must:

- Have a single responsibility.
- Protect its own state.
- Constructors should enforce valid object creation.
- Avoid parameterless public constructors unless required by Entity Framework.
- Avoid public setters whenever possible.
- Expose behavior instead of data.

Bad

```csharp
user.Status = UserStatus.Active;
```

Good

```csharp
user.Activate();
```

---

# 7. Database Rules

Database: PostgreSQL

Provider: Supabase

ORM: Entity Framework Core

---

Primary Keys

UUID

UUIDs are generated by the application using `Guid.NewGuid()`.

Do not rely on database-generated UUIDs.

---

Dates

Use DateTimeOffset.

Database type:

TIMESTAMPTZ

---

Soft Delete

Do not use soft delete columns.

Deletion must be handled through Status fields where applicable.

---

Enums

Database

SMALLINT

Backend

Enum

---

Configuration

Use EF Core conventions by default, matching the TMS API persistence style.

Do not create per-entity configuration classes merely to repeat conventions.
Use minimal Fluent API only where a documented constraint, relationship, index,
precision, conversion, or delete behavior cannot be represented correctly by conventions.

---

# 8. Repository Strategy

Do NOT use Generic Repository.

Entity Framework already provides repository behavior.
`DbContext` already implements the Unit of Work pattern.

Application handlers access persistence through `IApplicationDbContext`.
Infrastructure implements that contract with `AppDbContext`.

Do NOT introduce a separate `IUnitOfWork` or mandatory repository per entity.

Repositories should only contain domain-specific queries.

Business logic must never be implemented inside repositories.

Create repositories only when domain-specific queries are required.

Example

```
IUserRepository
IEventRepository
```

---

# 9. CQRS

The Application layer follows CQRS.

Structure

```
Application

Features

Users

Commands

Queries
```

Each feature contains

- Commands
- Queries
- Validators
- Handlers
- DTOs

---

# 10. Validation

Use FluentValidation.

Never perform validation inside Controllers.

---

# 11. Mapping

Use Mapster.

Mappings should remain inside the Application layer.

The Domain layer must never depend on mapping libraries.

Do not use AutoMapper.

---

# 12. Authentication

Authentication

Phone + OTP

Authorization

JWT

Refresh Token

Refresh Tokens must be stored in

```
user_sessions
```

Never store Refresh Tokens inside the users table.

---

# 13. API

Controllers should remain thin.

Controllers should never contain business logic.

Controllers should only

- Receive Request
- Call MediatR
- Return Response

---

# 14. Error Handling

Use Global Exception Middleware.

Do not wrap every controller inside try/catch.

---

# 15. Logging

Use Serilog.

Log

- Exceptions
- Warnings
- Authentication
- Critical Operations

Do not log sensitive information.

Never log

- OTP
- JWT
- Refresh Token
- Passwords

Every log entry should provide meaningful context.

## Avoid logging messages without useful diagnostic information.

# 16. Dependency Injection

Each layer owns its own registration.

Example

```
Application

DependencyInjection.cs
```

```
Infrastructure

DependencyInjection.cs
```

---

# 17. Result Pattern

Use Result Pattern.

Avoid throwing exceptions for expected business scenarios.

Example

```
Result

Result<T>
```

---

# 18. MediatR

Every request must go through MediatR.

Controllers must never directly call repositories.

---

# 19. File Storage

Files are stored in Supabase Storage.

Database stores only file paths.

Never store binary data inside PostgreSQL.

---

# 20. Code Style

Prefer explicit code over clever code.

Avoid deeply nested logic.

Keep methods short.

Prefer early return.

Avoid magic strings.

Use constants.

---

# 21. Comments

Write self-explanatory code.

Avoid unnecessary comments.

Only comment when explaining business intent.

---

# 22. Performance

Avoid unnecessary allocations.

Avoid loading unnecessary navigation properties.

Always use projections when possible.

Use pagination for collection endpoints.

## Prefer AsNoTracking() for read-only queries.

# 23. Security

Always validate input.

Never trust entity identifiers received from the client without authorization checks.

Never trust client-side data.

Never expose internal exceptions.

Always authorize protected endpoints.

---

# 24. Cursor Rules

When implementing a task:

- Do not modify unrelated files.
- Do not introduce new architecture.
- Do not generate sample code.
- Do not create placeholder implementations.
- Do not guess requirements.
- If information is missing, stop and explain why.

Follow this document strictly before implementing any task.

# 25. Architecture Decisions

The following architectural decisions are fixed for this project.

- Clean Architecture
- CQRS
- MediatR
- Entity Framework Core
- Convention-first EF Core persistence
- Mapster
- PostgreSQL
- Supabase
- Phone + OTP Authentication
- JWT Authentication
- Refresh Token Sessions
- UUID Primary Keys
- Result Pattern

Do not replace these technologies unless explicitly requested.
